<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ansgar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/801">801</a>–865</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ansgar, Apostle of the North</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ansgar was a Frankish monk and missionary, known as the Apostle of the North. Trained at Corbie and Corvey, he preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, where he founded the first church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a>. He became archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> in 831 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> sack of Hamburg in 845, head of the united see of Hamburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ansgar.jpg" alt="Ansgar" width="320" height="730" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siegfried Detlev Bendixen / After Hans Bornemann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bendixen_Ansgar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 832 it became the seat of a missionary archbishopric under <b>Ansgar</b>, the apostle of the North, but Vikings sacked the settlement in 845 and the see moved to Bremen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne made it the seat of a bishopric in 787, with the Anglo-Saxon missionary Willehad as first bishop, and after Vikings sacked Hamburg in 845 Archbishop <b>Ansgar</b> moved to Bremen, which became the seat of the archbishops of Hamburg-Bremen, leaders of the mission to Scandinavia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The monk <b>Ansgar</b> preached in Denmark and Sweden in the 9th century with little lasting success.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Frankish monk <b>Ansgar</b> preached at Birka about 830.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Rimbert's life of <b>Ansgar</b> describes a Swedish king attacking their stronghold at Apuolė in 854, and they appear in Norse sagas as both trading partners and raiders.</blockquote><a href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a></li><li><blockquote>Rimbert's life of <b>Ansgar</b> records King Björn receiving the Frankish missionary around 830 and King Olof receiving him again around 852.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
